INSERT INTO feature VALUES('rust-semantics-thir','runtime','Rust semantics at THIR','foreign-calls','partial','uncommon','dependent provers','A Rust function''s THIR is data and its dynamics a weakest-precondition calculus over it (Farka et al., Corten, 2026): the calculus is the specification monad of THIR''s control effect, one postcondition for a value and one for a `return`, a function''s guard its handler. Corten''s `example`, an early `return` inside `identity`''s argument, meets its specification at every value and every postcondition given `identity`''s, a fold computing each precondition and the kernel replaying it. The axioms are also a theory''s second-order rules over THIR''s nodes, which the checker orients and joins and an inspected call fires: `example`''s guarded body at false normalizes to its postcondition at zero. Corten''s buddy allocator''s `pop` meets its free-list specification over a value forest: a read or a write through a place is a value with its equation to the place, the list''s links are equations on block reads, and the proof takes 204 lines against Corten''s 2800 for the whole allocator. `std.thir` holds the data and the calculus. The reader of a Rust crate stages each requested function''s THIR in its record: an integer literal, the parameter, `if`, `return` and a call of a named function, any other node refused by its name and a call outside the request by its path. A signature with no body takes a function''s staged THIR as its value under a `thir` mark, and the `item` mark before each constructor of the item type states the path of the Rust item that the constructor stands for. Corten''s two functions, staged from their Rust crate, meet their specifications by the proofs of the bodies written by hand. A mark that stages no body is refused by name. `check:rust-record` settles the staged sources under the reader''s tests, because only it builds the reader.',NULL,'corpus/model/interop/thir/example.gandr corpus/pathological/interop/thir/example.gandr corpus/model/interop/thir/buddy-pop.gandr corpus/pathological/interop/thir/buddy-pop.gandr','corpus/ledger/features/rust-semantics-thir.gandr','#327');
